  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather will continue through the rest of the \nweek with a day to day warming trend.\n\nA strong mid/upper-level ridge will continue to dominate the \ncentral and eastern CONUS through the end of the week. Strong \nsurface ridging will continue to affect Western and Central NY \nas the embedded high pressure center remains parked in vicinity \nof the Mid-Atlantic states through Friday and then shifts toward\nNew England and eventually Atlantic waters near Nova Scotia \nFriday night through Saturday. Strong subsidence and dry air \nassociated with the high pressure system, surface and aloft, \nwill continue to support dry weather through at least Saturday \nnight. Ongoing warm advection will also bring temperatures to \nwell above average for the rest of the week. Saturday still \nlooks to be the warmest day as boundary layer flow turns more \nsoutherly on the synoptic scale, eliminates lake cooling, and \nintroduces downslope flow and associated compressional warming. \nHighs may reach the mid to upper 60's on the lake plains of \nWestern NY. These readings are close to 20F above average.\n\nA mid/upper-level trough and associated cold front should move \neastward across the Great Lakes Sunday through Monday and be \nfollowed by a ridge, surface and aloft, building from the\nnorth-central CONUS and vicinity by Monday night into Tuesday. \nModel guidance continues to trend a little slower with the cold \nfront passage, although there is still some timing spread in \nensemble members. If the slower timing holds, the showers \nassociated with the cold front may hold off until Sunday night \ninto Monday for Western and Central NY. The cold front will \nusher in a much cooler air mass early next week with the \npotential for below average high temperatures in the lower 30's \nto near 40F this Tuesday afternoon. The post-front air mass \nshould be cold enough and may be moist enough to support lake- \neffect rain and/or snow showers generally southeast and south of\nthe eastern Great Lakes Monday into Tuesday.",
